swer. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Structure should be easy for listeners to track; credit at least one source aloud. Reasoning: I used present by attending to research and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Ignoring audience questions after inviting them is poor presenting manners. Correction: Structure should be easy for listeners to track; credit at least one source aloud. Why: the correction restores accurate use of present.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Ignoring audience questions after inviting them is poor presenting manners. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating present on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with present, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Structure should be easy for listeners to track; credit at least one source aloud. This matches the lesson skill for present.","options":["Ignoring audience questions after inviting them is poor presenting manners.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Structure should be easy for listeners to track; credit at least one source aloud."],"question":"Plan an opener, three fact cues and a closing thanks for a 90-second talk. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Ignoring audienc