one source aloud.","explain":"Presenting the research project means speaking clearly from cue cards or slides, crediting sources and answering a simple audience question.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Ignoring audience questions after inviting them is poor presenting manners. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Research presentation map with spoken credit.","caption":"Invite and answer one audience question.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Talk map</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">question; 3 facts; credit; invite question</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Credit aloud</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">According to...</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: I state my question, share three facts with a slide visual, say \"According to...\" for one source and invite one question Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: I state my question, share three facts with a slide visual, say \"According to...\" for one source and invite one question Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about present?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nI state my question, share three facts with a slide visual, say \"According to...\" for one source and invite one question at the end. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep 