ners. Here is a more productive model: I state my question, share three facts with a slide visual, say \"According to...\" for one source and invite one question at the end.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStructure should be easy for listeners to track; credit at least one source aloud.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Ignoring audience questions after inviting them is poor presenting manners. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about present?","type":"multiple_choice"},"exa